WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.025
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book three.

2
00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:09.106
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.406 --> 00:00:15.762
Unit twelve: Memory Studies. Today you will explain why societies remember with 'so that' purpose clauses.

4
00:00:16.062 --> 00:00:18.703
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.807
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.930
Collective memory.

7
00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:29.484
Collective memory means the shared way a group remembers its past.

8
00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:32.756
The war shaped Cambodia's collective memory.

9
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.269
Your turn. Say: Collective memory.

10
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:40.645
Collective memory.

11
00:00:41.383 --> 00:00:42.442
Commemoration.

12
00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.146
Commemoration means a public act of remembering.

13
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.423
A commemoration is held every 20 May.

14
00:00:50.017 --> 00:00:53.006
Your turn. Say: Commemoration.

15
00:00:55.867 --> 00:00:57.019
Commemoration.

16
00:00:57.783 --> 00:00:58.738
Monument.

17
00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:03.445
Monument means a structure built to remember a person or event.

18
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.723
The Independence Monument stands in Phnom Penh.

19
00:01:07.317 --> 00:01:09.725
Your turn. Say: Monument.

20
00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:13.637
Monument.

21
00:01:14.383 --> 00:01:15.152
Trauma.

22
00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.879
Trauma means deep emotional harm caused by a terrible experience.

23
00:01:20.483 --> 00:01:22.889
Trauma can pass between generations.

24
00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:25.962
Your turn. Say: Trauma.

25
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.557
Trauma.

26
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:31.434
Remembrance.

27
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.063
Remembrance means the act of honouring and remembering the dead.

28
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:38.464
A day of remembrance was declared.

29
00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:41.714
Your turn. Say: Remembrance.

30
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:45.800
Remembrance.

31
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:47.505
Erasure.

32
00:01:48.517 --> 00:01:52.095
Erasure means the removal of something from memory or records.

33
00:01:52.683 --> 00:01:56.169
Erasure of minority history is a form of injustice.

34
00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:58.948
Your turn. Say: Erasure.

35
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:02.836
Erasure.

36
00:02:03.583 --> 00:02:04.596
Narrative.

37
00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:09.104
Narrative means the story a society tells about its past.

38
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:12.703
Official narratives may hide uncomfortable facts.

39
00:02:13.317 --> 00:02:16.027
Your turn. Say: Narrative.

40
00:02:18.867 --> 00:02:19.786
Narrative.

41
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:21.516
Legacy.

42
00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:25.677
Legacy means what the past leaves for later generations.

43
00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:28.794
The legacy of the genocide is still felt.

44
00:02:29.383 --> 00:02:32.187
Your turn. Say: Legacy.

45
00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:35.849
Legacy.

46
00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:40.725
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:41.583 --> 00:02:47.486
Visal, you visited the Tuol Sleng Genocide Museum. Why do you think it was preserved, rather than demolished?

48
00:02:48.583 --> 00:02:57.154
It was preserved so that the crimes of the Khmer Rouge would never be forgotten, and so that future generations could learn from them.

49
00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.412
Isn't it painful for survivors?

50
00:03:01.517 --> 00:03:10.334
Very. That's why commemoration must be handled with care, so that victims are honoured rather than turned into a spectacle.

51
00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.357
What role does education play?

52
00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:25.784
Since 2009, the genocide has been taught in schools so that young people understand their parents' trauma, which helps prevent erasure and denial.

53
00:03:26.933 --> 00:03:28.596
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:32.344
Grammar focus: Purpose clauses (so that).

55
00:03:32.644 --> 00:03:33.854
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:37.420
Monuments are built so that events are not forgotten.

57
00:03:38.317 --> 00:03:41.744
The prison was preserved so that future generations could learn.

58
00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:46.786
History is taught in schools so that young people understand their parents' trauma.

59
00:03:47.733 --> 00:03:56.434
'So that' introduces a full clause of purpose, often with a modal: so that they can / could / will / would. Use it instead of 'to' when the subject of the purpose is different from the main subject.

60
00:03:57.183 --> 00:04:01.496
Be careful! Don't say: Monuments are built so that we don't forgetting.

61
00:04:03.433 --> 00:04:06.745
Say: Monuments are built so that we don't forget.

62
00:04:07.045 --> 00:04:08.870
'So that' + full clause.

63
00:04:09.667 --> 00:04:12.110
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:14.505
Why Societies Remember.

65
00:04:14.805 --> 00:04:20.717
Memory studies examines how communities remember, interpret and sometimes forget their past.

66
00:04:21.733 --> 00:04:26.786
Monuments and museums are created so that significant events remain visible in public life.

67
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:34.188
However, collective memory is never neutral; governments may highlight some events so that others are quietly forgotten.

68
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:42.076
In post-conflict societies, survivors' testimony is often recorded so that their experiences cannot be denied.

69
00:04:43.067 --> 00:04:48.654
Remembering painful histories is therefore both an act of mourning and a commitment to prevent repetition.

70
00:04:49.767 --> 00:04:53.873
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:54.717 --> 00:04:57.332
The names were recorded so that they blank forgotten.

72
00:04:57.632 --> 00:05:01.482
A, would not be. B, not be. C, would not.

73
00:05:05.033 --> 00:05:07.300
The answer is A: would not be.

74
00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:10.252
Choose the correct sentence.

75
00:05:10.552 --> 00:05:17.305
A, They built it so that people remember.. B, They built it so that to remember.. C, They built it so people to remember..

76
00:05:20.867 --> 00:05:24.155
The answer is A: They built it so that people remember..

77
00:05:25.317 --> 00:05:27.688
A public act of remembering is a blank.

78
00:05:27.988 --> 00:05:31.908
A, commemoration. B, trauma. C, erasure.

79
00:05:35.467 --> 00:05:37.594
The answer is A: commemoration.

80
00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.725
Removing something from memory or records is blank.

81
00:05:42.025 --> 00:05:45.806
A, narrative. B, erasure. C, legacy.

82
00:05:49.367 --> 00:05:51.157
The answer is B: erasure.

83
00:05:52.317 --> 00:05:55.001
According to the reading, collective memory is…

84
00:05:55.301 --> 00:06:00.510
A, always neutral. B, never neutral. C, decided by survivors only.

85
00:06:04.067 --> 00:06:06.298
The answer is B: never neutral.

86
00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:10.919
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:11.783 --> 00:06:16.279
Why might a society choose to build a monument to remember a difficult event?

88
00:06:33.083 --> 00:06:34.665
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:34.965 --> 00:07:07.567
A society might build a monument to a difficult event so that the victims are honoured and the lessons of the past remain visible. In Cambodia, the memorial at Choeung Ek was created so that the world would not forget the crimes of the Khmer Rouge. Such places also give families somewhere to grieve when many victims have no graves. Some people feel that remembering painful events keeps old wounds open. However, I believe that forgetting is more dangerous, because it allows history to repeat itself.

90
00:07:07.867 --> 00:07:09.472
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:10.383 --> 00:07:14.719
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and purpose clauses (so that).

92
00:07:15.019 --> 00:07:20.051
Today's value is remembrance. Remember the victims, so that history is never repeated.

93
00:07:20.351 --> 00:07:22.876
See you in Unit thirteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:23.176 --> 00:07:26.571
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

